\1
   CE18 OVERVIEW

   This software is used to program configuration and
   channel data to the Vertex VX-300 portable transceiver.

   On-line help is available by pressing F1 at any of
   the main menu entry fields, or on any of the submenus.

   CE18 Features

	. Mouse support.  Please ensure that your mouse
	  driver is installed correctly.
	
	. The status bar along the bottom of the screen
	  contains information such as function of the
	  F keys, short help explanations, action keys or
	  ranges of data entry.

	. Many of the hardware parameters are initialized
	  to default values.  Make changes to these items
	  only for custom applications.

\2
   OPEN FILE

   This menu item allows one to open existing files with
   information of VX-300 configurations.

   <Path>      Use the mouse or press <ALT>-P to select
		 the <Path> window.  Specify the directory
		 path of which you wish to retrieve the
		 file.

   <Name>      Use the mouse or press <ALT>-N to select
		 the <Name> window.  Provide a search
		 pattern using wildcards <*> to obtain a
		 set of files you wish to select.  The
		 default search string is "*.V10".

		 Optionally, you can enter the exact file
		 name you wish to open and press <ENTER>
		 to open the file.

   <Files - #> Use the mouse or press <ALT>-F to select
		 the <Files> window.  This window lists all
		 subdirectories (indicated by a square
		 prefix) and files under the directory path
		 which match the search pattern.  The #
		 indicates the number of files that match
		 the search string.

		 Use the Up/Down keys to select the
		 subdirectory/file you wish to open, and
		 press <ENTER>.  If you wish to cancel
		 opening the file, press <ESC>.

\3
   SAVE FILE

   This menu item allows one to save the configuration
   information displayed on screen.

   <Path>      Use the mouse or press <ALT>-P to select
		 the <Path> window.  Specify the directory
		 path of which you wish to save the
		 file.

   <Files - #> Use the mouse or press <ALT>-F to select
		 the <Files> window.  This window lists all
		 subdirectories (indicated by a square
		 prefix) and files under the directory path
		 which match the search pattern.  Use the
		 Up/Down keys to select the subdirectory/
		 file you wish to save the configuration
		 in, and press <ENTER>.  Press <ESC> to
		 cancel.

   <Name>      Use the mouse or press <ALT>-N to select
		 the <Name> window.  Enter the file name
		 you wish to save the configuration in and
		 press <ENTER>.  Press <ESC> to cancel.
\4
   PRINT

   Choosing this submenu item prints a hard copy of the
   configuration data presently displayed on screen.

   Make sure that the printer port is set correctly by
   specifying the correct port in the CONFIGURE submenu.

\5
   CONFIGURE

   Color/monochrome display can be chosen in this
   submenu.  Communication ports to interface the radio,
   and printer ports to print configuration data can
   also be set.

   NOTE: MAKE SURE TO SELECT THE CORRECT COMMUNICATIONS
	 PORT TO ENSURE PROPER OPERATION.  THE PROGRAM
	 WILL LOCK-UP IF THEIR IS A CONFLICT BETWEEN THE
	 MOUSE PORT AND CT-42 PORT.

\6
   EXIT

   This command terminates the program.  Make sure to
   save any necessary configuration data prior to
   exiting the program.

\7
   HARDWARE PARAMETERS

   These parameters are related to the hardware of the
   VX-300 radio, and should be handled with care.
   Parameters to set the operating frequency band,
   channel spacing, power save off time, and DCS RX/TX
   data polarity included in this category.

\8
   FREQ. CONTROL

   These parameters allow user to configure the freq.
   control mode of the radio unit.
   
\9
   COMMON

   These parameters are used to configure the common
   settings of the radio unit.

\10
   POWER SAVING PARAMETERS

   These parameters are used to configure the power
   saving functions of the radio unit.

\11
   DTMF CODES

   This will allow user to enter up to 10 DTMF numbers 
   for the radio unit. Each number can accept up to 15 
   DTMF digits.
   
\12
   CHANNEL ALLOCATION

   Allows the user to allocate any number of channels to
   any or all groups, uo to 99 channels distributed
   among uo to 9 groups. Always allocate channels to
   groups in ascending group order. Do not skip over
   unused (0 channel) groups.

\13
   MR MODE

   Governs functions used in the Memory Recall mode.
   
\14
   MISCELLANEOUS

   Programmable variables that cannot be included in any
   other category are gathered under this submenu.  These
   parameters should be used to customize the radio to
   your application needs.

\15
   HOME CHANNEL

   Programs channel data for the Home Channel.
   
\16
   VFO-A

   Programs channel data for the VFO-A.
   
\17
   VFO-B

   Programs channel data for the VFO-B.

\18
   UPLOAD

   Used to read information from the VX-300 raido to the
   computer.  Existing data on screen will be overwritten
   with the data of the radio unless properly saved.

   NOTE: MAKE SURE TO SELECT THE CORRECT COMMUNICATIONS
	 PORT TO ENSURE PROPER OPERATION.  THE PROGRAM
	 WILL LOCK-UP IF THEIR IS A CONFLICT BETWEEN THE
	 MOUSE PORT AND CT-42 PORT.

\19
   DOWNLOAD

   Used to write configuration data from the computer to
   the connected VX-300 radio.  Data will be verified
   for integrity by the program before downloading is
   initiated.
   
   NOTE: MAKE SURE TO SELECT THE CORRECT COMMUNICATIONS
	 PORT TO ENSURE PROPER OPERATION.  THE PROGRAM
	 WILL LOCK-UP IF THEIR IS A CONFLICT BETWEEN THE
	 MOUSE PORT AND CT-42 PORT.

\23
   CPU CLOCK SHIFT

   Putting a check mark for each channel, (by pressing 
   ENTER key), will shift the CTCSS crystal frequency
   during transmit mode. Usually, CE14 will set/reset
   the check mark automatically after transmit frequency
   is entered.
    
\50
   FREQ BAND

   Displays the frequency band of the radio.

\51
   REF XTAL

   Do not alter this setting after the frequency band
   selection has automatically set the value unless you
   are performing special modifications to the radio.

\52
   INTERMEDIATE FREQUENCY

   Do not alter this setting after the frequency band
   selection has automatically set the value unless you
   are performing special modifications to the radio.

\53
   LOCAL OFFSET

   Do not alter this setting after the frequency band
   selection has automatically set the value unless you
   are performing special modifications to the radio.

   Change this setting to eliminate image problems in
   some service areas.  Radio will have to be retuned
   after modification.  This information is used in
   conjunction with IF, REF OSC FREQ and channel
   information to compute divide by numbers.


\70
   LIGHTING

   When ON, the display lamp and busy/TX lamps will
   illuminate when appropriate. When OFF, the lamps
   will never illuminate. 

\71
   BEEP

   When ON, the radio beeps when keypad buttons are
   pressed.
   
\72
   TONE LOCK OUT

   This will allow the user to choose between OFF
   (normal PTT operation), BCLO (PTT is inhibited when
   there is a signal on the receive frequency), and
   BTLO (PTT is inhibited when there is a signal on
   the receive frequency unless that signal has the
   correct decode CTCSS).
   
\73
   BELL

   When ON, the bell will ring when the correct CTCSS
   is decoded. When OFF, the bell will not ring.
   
\74
   LOCK STATUS

   When ON, the radio will power-on with the lock-
   selected item(s) locked. When OFF, the radio will
   power-on unlocked.
   
\75
   PTT LOCK

   When ON, the PTT will be locked when the lock
   function is turned on.

\76
   KEY LOCK

   When ON, the front panel keys will be locked when
   the lock function is turned on.

\77
   DIAL LOCK

   When ON, the dial (rotary channel selector) will be
   locked when the lock function is turned on.

\78
   LAMP MODE

   When Key ON, the display lamp will illuminate when
   any button is pressed, and will remain on for 5
   seconds. When 5 seconds, the display lamp will
   illuminate for 5 seconds only when the Lamp button
   is pressed. When Toggle, the display lamp will
   illuminate only when the Lamp button is pressed,
   and will remain on indefinitely until the Lamp
   button is pressed again.
   function is turned on.

\79
   SQUELCH LEVEL

   Set the squelch threshold level. 0 is minimum, 8 is
   maximum.

\90
   TRANSMIT TIMER

   Set the TX timeout time. Minimum is 1 min., and 
   maximum is 10 mins.

\91
   APO TIME MODE

   Automatic Power Off. Radio will shut off if there is
   no TX or button activity for the amount of time
   programmed. Choices are OFF, 0.5 hr., 1.0 hr., or 8
   hrs. 

\92
   RX SAVE MODE

   Sets the timing ratio of the RX battery saver.
   Choices are OFF (no save activity), ABS, and 1:1
   (minimum save) thru 1:10 (maximun save). ABS is
   Automatic Battery Save. When ABS is selected, the
   radio will automatically select the most optimum
   save ratio based on recent operating activity.


\102
   SKIP

   When enabled, allows the user to delete channels
   from the scan list.
    
\103
   REVERSE

   When enabled, allows the user to invoke the reverse
   function (radio transmits on the receive frequency
   and receives on the transmit frequency).
    
\104
   SCAN

   When enabled, allows the user to scan.
    
\105
   DUAL WATCH

   When enabled, allows the user to use the Dual Watch
   function.
   
\106
   HI/LOW

   When enabled, allows the user to switch between High
   and Low transmit power.

\107
   DTMF

   When enabled, allows the user to transmit manual
   DTMF signals from the key pad.
   
\108
   DTMF MR

   When enabled, allows the user to transmit the pre-
   programmed DTMF autodial string(s).


\120
   SUB FREQUENCY DISPLAY
    
   When ON, the actual operating frequency is display
   in the right half of the display.

\121
   SCAN STOP RESUME
    
   When in carrier, the radio will pause and remain on
   an active received signal until the signal drops. 
   When in 5 Sec., the radio will pause on an active
   received signal for 5 seconds, and will then resume
   scanning even if the received signal is still
   present.

\122
   ANI FUNCTION

   When enabled, the radio will transmit the programmed
   ANI string each time the PTT is pressed.

\123
   DTMF MEMORY MODE

   When ON, allows the user to program DTMF strings from
   keypad.

\124
   MEMORY ONLY MODE

   When ON, the user can not switch to VFO mode.

\125
   DUAL WATCH

   When ON, the radio will power-on in the Dual Watch
   mode.

\126
   DTMF SEQ. OUT

   When enabled, allows the user to string together
   and send multiple DTMF memory string.


\300
   CH#

   This column indicates the absolute channel number. For
   example; if there are 10 channels in Group 1, Group 2
   will start at channel 11. Press the space bar in here
   will toggle between channel locked (line appears in
   gray) and unlocked (line appears in blue) conditions.

\301
   CHANNEL ALPHA TAGS

   Enter a 4 character alphanumeric channel name here.
   This name will appear on the left half of the radio's
   display when this channel is selected.
   
\302
   RECEIVE FREQUENCY

   This entry will except the receive frequency which
   should be a multiple of the Spacing shown at the top
   of the screen. In this column you can directly enter
   the exact receive frequency or press ENTER and use the
   Arrow keys to edit any digit within the field. Press
   ENTER to accept the frequency.
   
\303
   TRANSMIT FREQUENCY

   When RPT Shift shows DUP, you can directly enter the
   exact transmit frequency or press ENTER and use the
   Arrow keys to edit any digit within the field. Press
   ENTER to accept the frequency. This entry may be
   programmed to shift automatically to any offset of
   the receive frequency. The offset is programmed in
   the TX Offset column.

\304
   TRANSMIT OFFSET

   Can be edit only when RPT Shift shows "-" or "+".
   Enter the amount of offset (MHz.KHz) desired from
   the Receive Frequency.
   
\305
   REPEATER SHIFT

   Select the offset for Transmit Frequency. SIMP means
   radio will transmit on the receive frequency. "-"
   means radio will transmit on a frequency that is
   below the receive frequency the amount specified in
   TX Offset. "+" means radio will transmit on a
   frequency that is above the receive frequency the
   amount specified in TX Offset. DUP means radio will
   transmit on the frequency shown in the TX Freq.
   column. Can not be edited when displayed in gray.  

\306
   CTCSS DECODE

   In this column you will select the CTCSS tone to be
   decoded on this channel. This can be done by pressing
   the space bar, then scrolling the list of tones(CTCSS)
   with the arrow keys. When you have select the correct
   tone or code, press ENTER to accept. Make sure that
   your radio is equipped with the CTCSS decode unit.
   
\307
   CTCSS ENCODE

   In this column you will select the CTCSS tone to be
   encoded on this channel. This can be done by pressing
   the space bar, then scrolling the list of tones(CTCSS)
   with the arrow keys. When you have select the correct
   tone or code, press ENTER to accept.
   
\308
   CHANNEL SCAN

   Press <SPACE> to select scan stop (a check mark
   shows) or scan skip (s dash shows) for this channel.

\309
   POWER MODE

   Press <SPACE> to select Hi (5 watts) or Lo (1 watt)
   transmit power.

\310
   CHANNEL STEP

   Press <SPACE> to select desired channel step size.

\311
   CPU CLOCK SHIFT

   Press <SPACE> to select shift (a check mark shows) or
   normal (a dash shows) for this channel. Changing the
   clock shift setting can usually eliminate a parasitic
   oscillation ("birdie") on a problem RX frequency.
